592186552374 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552374 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552374 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552374 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552374 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2ae6c02-1c78-5b5d-926b-2d33957e08c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552430 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+(2+x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4696ec15-27d3-55f9-a25e-60bb43e6e055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552430 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}=8{sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2+x=2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8{sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}16{u}^{3}+16u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552492 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2316759-1f4d-59b3-aebb-24c5b1ac8267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552492 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-xy=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt=du,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}6{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552504 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xi+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c696854b-d1cc-50f5-8398-3fba96da4739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552504 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552541 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552541 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1da0af94-7ee7-5f6d-819f-b12e5fd6862d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552541 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f3daaae-7b86-5566-920d-102af6de28ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552564 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552